hey might bluff or value-bet light. But since they haven’t bet, checking keeps pot controlled.  \n- **Small Raise (e.g., 100)**: Could represent strength, possibly inducing a fold from weaker hands (like J-J, 9-9, or A-high with no pair). But risks scaring away worse hands that might call a check.  \n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill. Too aggressive given the board texture and prior passive play. Likely folds worse, called by better (if any).  \n- **Fold (-1)**: Not justified. You have a pair of Queens — too strong to fold without clear evidence of superior hand.\n\nReason:  \nYou have **top pair, medium kicker**, on a relatively dry board. The absence of betting from your opponent on the turn and river after raising on the flop suggests they may have had a draw that missed or a weaker hand trying to build the pot. Now they appear weak. Checking here is safe and likely leads to showdown. A small raise could extract value from very weak hands, but given the prior passivity, even calling a bet would be questionable — so betting may not get called by worse often. Therefore, **checking to control risk and go to showdown is optimal**.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
